          "Writing aims for fidelity, clarity and elegance, in that order. ",
          "Fidelity preserves the meaning: actors, conditions, responsibilities and limits stay intact, facts keep their sources, and counterexamples, uncertainty and failure stay visible. Clarity begins with accurate understanding, then puts it plainly and fluently, so the reader can understand and act. Elegance brings taste and aesthetic judgment to rhythm, transitions and form, with precise verbs and concrete nouns, without slogans or pretended depth. ",
          "If collection ran two hours late, write two hours late, not “roughly on time”. That is fidelity. Lead with the conclusion and the decision needed, then the detail. That is clarity. ",
          "Clarity has concrete checks: a title that names the subject and purpose; the conclusion first; one point per paragraph; important terms defined at first use. Before sending, ask whether a reader outside the work could restate the conclusion, basis, limits and next action. ",
